# Evenly spaced hues: scale_hue

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

diamonds = Giggly.Datasets.diamonds()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_colour_hue()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_hue.html), which covers
`scale_fill_hue()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`scale_colour_hue()` maps each value of a discrete column to a hue evenly spaced around the colour
wheel, all at one chroma and luminance. Its options choose the range of hues, the chroma and the
luminance, where on the wheel to start and which way round to go, and the colour of missing values;
the palettes it makes are not safe for readers with colour blindness. `scale_fill_hue()` does the
same for fill. Giggly colours a discrete column with these hues by default, a missing value in
grey50, and an ordered factor with viridis, as ggplot2 does, and reads them back with a legend,
and has `Giggly.Plot.scale_color_hue/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_fill_hue/2`, with the range of
hues, the chroma and the luminance spelt out (`hue:`, `chroma:`, `luminance:`), the direction and
the missing value's colour, but not where on the wheel to start (`h.start`). A plot is described
with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell
aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot
and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. It binds ggplot2's `diamonds`,
`Giggly.Datasets.diamonds/0`, to `diamonds`, ggplot2's name for it.

## A sample of diamonds

The examples draw a random sample of the `diamonds` dataset, picked after `set.seed(596)`.
`clarity` is an ordered factor, which ggplot2 colours with viridis by default rather than with
hues.

```r
set.seed(596)
dsamp <- diamonds[sample(nrow(diamonds), 1000), ]
(d <- ggplot(dsamp, aes(carat, price)) + geom_point(aes(colour = clarity)))
```

R's `sample()` picks the rows at random, the same ones every time after `set.seed(596)`. The cell
writes out the 1,000 row numbers it picks, counted from 1 as R counts rows, and takes those rows of
every column, as `diamonds[rows, ]` does. A `Giggly.Factor` enumerates as its values alone, so
`cut`, `color` and `clarity` are rebuilt with `Giggly.Factor.new/2`, keeping their levels and
their order.

